  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure centered over the Lower Great Lakes region will\ncontinue to promote dry weather through Wednesday. Visible\nsatellite at the time of writing displays afternoon cumulus \nfields across the north-central lowlands that should gradually\nfade before sunset this evening. Otherwise, should see\nstrengthening low level easterly winds tonight that could impose\nbreezy winds along the mountains as early as midnight that will\ntriumph into the day Wednesday.\n\nSlightly moderating temperatures are progged for Wednesday\nafternoon, with climate sites briefly returning to their \nclimatological norms. Should also begin to see an uptick in mid\nto upper level clouds as moisture pulls northward ahead of a\nstrengthening disturbance, messaged more in detail below.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA strengthening disturbance off the Carolina coast will bring a\ncornucopia of weather concerns to the area stretching from\nThanksgiving into the start of the weekend. The evolution of\nthis system begins today into Wednesday, with shortwave energy\nshifting poleward off the Florida coast and phasing with a wave\nstemming from a parent upper level trough. \n\nAs the system intensifies, the attached rain shield will \nstretch from the eastern seaboard and as far westward as \nIllinois, and will lift northward late Wednesday evening into \nearly Thursday morning. Widespread precipitation, falling \ninitially as all rain will encroach the southern coalfields and \nsoutheastern foothills during the overnight hours and fill in\nnorthward by Thanksgiving morning. The highest likelihood of\nPoPs engulfs the entirety of the forecast area through much of\nthe day Thursday before the system shifts northward overnight.\nThis will be around the time that the nearly stacked disturbance\nwill reach full maturity, encompassing much of the northeastern\nportions of the country. \n\nIn the midst of remaining as all rain, forecast QPF amounts \nwill be nearing an inch in most places across the Central \nAppalachians, a slight uptick from previous forecast cycles.\nWhile forecast amounts don't appear too menacing, could still\nsee localized high water issues sprouting in troublesome spots,\nespecially those that observe blocked culverts from leaf litter.\nCould also see a brief period of freezing rain mixing in along\nthe West Virginia and Virginia state line along the spine of the\nAppalachians early Thursday morning before returning to an all\nrain regime for the Thanksgiving holiday.\n\nBy early Friday morning, the center of the upper level low will\npass directly overhead or just north of the forecast area,\nsupplying a blast of colder temperatures. Sub-freezing\ntemperatures settling into the area will yield a transition over\nto a wintry mix for much of the area early in the morning into\nthe start of the afternoon, before daytime heating morphs\nprecipitation back to rain in the lower elevations. Elsewhere\nalong the higher terrain, snow showers will prevail and could\nculminate into light accumulations for our mountain zones\nthrough Friday evening.\n\nAdditionally, a tightened pressure gradient attached to this\nstrong system will yield gusty to strong winds across the area\nfrom late Wednesday night into the weekend. Peak forecast gusts\nnearing advisory thresholds were noted along the spine of the\nAppalachians, while the lowlands and surrounding foothills could\nsee gusts as strong as 20 to 30 miles per hour through the\ncourse of this event. In collaboration with neighboring offices,\nopted to hold off at least one more forecast cycle to monitor\npotential need of wind headlines.\n\nA return to drier, quieter weather is slated for a fleeting\nmoment on Saturday once this system embarks on a journey off the\nNew England coast. However, more active weather is on the\nhorizon for the end of the weekend into the start of next week.",
